<section class="sub" id="sft-housing">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">01 · PEOPLE &amp; HOUSING</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Who lives here, and what it costs them</h2>
</div>
<div class="card-grid">
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>Cost burden is the headline finding</h3></div>
<p>One-third of Santa Fe households spend more than 30% of income on housing; split by tenure, that's 39% of homeowners and 49% of renters. Housing variety and affordability are both named as limited in the Assessment Report, and the report characterizes recent gains in median income as reflecting displacement — lower-income households leaving — more than genuine poverty reduction.</p>
</article>
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>An aging, slowing population</h3></div>
<p>Santa Fe is older on average than peer cities, and residents aged 65–84 have more than doubled in the past 20 years. Population is forecast to peak around 2040 near 99,000 under a medium-growth scenario and decline after. Newcomers tend to be wealthier than existing residents, adding displacement pressure in legacy neighborhoods — most acutely near acequia-adjacent areas, per the report.</p>
</article>
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>Wages don't match the cost of living</h3></div>
<p>Wages in the sectors that employ the most Santa Feans — tourism, healthcare, education — lag the local cost of living. The report ties this directly to the housing-affordability finding: without wage growth or more housing supply at a range of price points, the gap keeps widening from both directions.</p>
</article>
</div>
</section>

<section class="sub" id="sft-land">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">02 · LAND USE &amp; ZONING</div>
<h2 class="section-title">What's already on the books, and what's already changing</h2>
</div>
<div class="card-grid">
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>Growth is concentrated on the south and west sides</h3></div>
<p>These areas need more infrastructure, parks, and services relative to demand, per the report. Current land use regulations are described as complex and restrictive, needing simplification to support both housing and economic growth. The Santa Fe Style — the dominant design identity — covers only part of the city; large areas south and west of the historic downtown are built around single-family housing and car travel, not the walkable-core pattern the Santa Fe Style is usually associated with.</p>
</article>
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>5,500 acres vacant, undercounting real capacity</h3></div>
<p>The Parcel Availability Inventory puts vacant, developable land in the city at roughly 5,500 acres, plus another 1,000 acres of underutilized land. The inventory's own value-based screening method underrepresents surface parking lots and vacant commercial properties — meaning actual redevelopment capacity, particularly in central locations, is likely higher than the headline number.</p>
</article>
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>The city's own LDC Phase 1 draft is already consolidating overlays</h3></div>
<p>Separate from the General Plan process, a draft "LDC Foundations Alignment" document already proposes eliminating the RS and ER overlay districts, folding new overlay creation into three existing categories (Historic, Neighborhood Conservation, Planned Unit Development), and relocating the Arts and Crafts Overlay District into a consolidated Neighborhood Mixed-Use District. This is a live, in-progress draft — not an adopted decision — but it signals the direction city staff are already moving the code, independent of whatever the General Plan update ultimately decides on land use designations.</p>
</article>
</div>
</section>

<section class="sub" id="sft-equity">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">03 · EQUITY ANALYSIS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">13 census tracts, identified by the city's own methodology</h2>
<p class="section-note">The Assessment Report's Equity Analysis chapter scores every census tract in the study area against three burden categories — household, transportation, and environmental health — each with several indicators, at the 90th percentile <em>relative to other Santa Fe-area tracts</em>, not national averages. A low-income tract with one or more indicators at that threshold is classified an Equity Community.</p>
</div>
<div class="card-grid">
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>Where they are, and who lives there</h3></div>
<p>The 13 identified Equity Community tracts are low-income and concentrated west of St. Francis Drive/Highway 285 and east of Veterans Memorial Highway/Highway 599. They have a higher share of Hispanic, Black, Indigenous, or other people of color residents than the county average, and the report notes a general correlation between ethnicity and lower-than-average income across these tracts.</p>
</article>
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>What burdens compound there</h3></div>
<p>Many of these tracts sit along high-traffic roadways, contain a disproportionate share of the city's mobile home parks and RV communities, and show higher unemployment, a higher share of residents with disabilities, and lower car ownership than other tracts. The Agua Fria neighborhood is specifically flagged as having the city's highest proximity to traffic.</p>
</article>
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>Transit doesn't reach the need</h3></div>
<p>Despite lower car ownership and higher transportation burden, Equity Community households still spend significant income on transportation — the report attributes this to transit not being a viable option for most of these households in practice. Santa Fe Trails ridership fell nearly 60% after 2019. The report reads this as a mismatch between where transit investment is needed and where current service runs, not as evidence that these residents don't need transit.</p>
</article>
</div>
</section>

<section class="sub" id="sft-mobility">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">04 · TRANSPORTATION &amp; WATER</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Two infrastructure systems under different kinds of strain</h2>
</div>
<div class="stat-row" style="margin-bottom:10px;">
<div class="stat"><b>~400k</b> annual Santa Fe Trails ridership</div>
<div class="stat"><b>1 route</b> at a useful frequency; the other 9 run 60+ min headways</div>
<div class="stat"><b>170+ mi</b> of trails</div>
<div class="stat"><b>87%</b> of 2023 water use from surface sources</div>
<div class="stat"><b>220 days/yr</b> the Santa Fe River now runs dry</div>
</div>
<p>Parking is described as overbuilt and underused in many areas, with modern parking-management strategies identified as an opportunity. Transportation investment is flagged as needing closer alignment with housing, economic development, and parks — i.e., treated as one system rather than funded and planned separately. On water: supply is described as well-managed today but vulnerable to long-term climate change and availability pressure; stormwater and wastewater systems need investment and better coordination across departments.</p>
</section>

<section class="sub" id="sft-1999">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">05 · THE 1999 PLAN'S RECORD</div>
<h2 class="section-title">What 25 years of the current General Plan actually produced</h2>
<p class="section-note">The Assessment Report's Policy Implications and Recommendations chapter reviews every policy in the 1999 General Plan against what the city says actually happened. Its own framing: "If everything is a priority, then nothing is a priority."</p>
</div>
<div class="card-grid">
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>Partial implementation was the norm, not the exception</h3></div>
<p>More than half of the 1999 plan's policies were only partially implemented or remain ongoing after 25 years; smaller shares were fully carried out or never addressed at all. Some policies proved difficult to quantify as "done" or "not done," and some are no longer relevant to current conditions.</p>
</article>
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>Specific items the city's own review flags as never implemented</h3></div>
<p>Neighborhood centers were never formally defined, despite being referenced repeatedly in the 1999 plan — all development has instead been governed by the Land Development Code's underlying base zoning. The infill zoning classification intended to create employment opportunities on small residential tracts was never implemented. A proposed incentive package for land-banking near neighborhood centers was not implemented. Most downtown parking-reform strategies from 1999 were never realized, leaving downtown reliant on car-oriented approaches the plan itself intended to move away from.</p>
</article>
<article class="card">
<div class="card-top"><h3>A recurring gap: no data, no inventory</h3></div>
<p>Across public safety, childcare, schools, and senior services, the review repeatedly cites the same limitation: a lack of consistent data and facilities inventories, which constrains long-term planning regardless of how clearly a policy was written in 1999.</p>
</article>
</div>
</section>

<section class="sub" id="sft-vision">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">06 · THE VISION STATEMENT</div>
<h2 class="section-title">The current official language, in full</h2>
<p class="section-note">Quoted directly from the July 2026 Vision Summary — the document the draft General Plan is meant to carry forward. See <a href="/recommendations/#rec-guide">General Plan Language Comparisons</a> for how this reads against other adopted plans' vision language.</p>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Mission —</b> "Faithful to our heritage, focused on our present and forging our future, Santa Fe Forward shapes the City Different, sets priorities and creates opportunities for all."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Unified Vision —</b> "Santa Fe supports and respects the needs and growth of all in harmony with our heritage and environment, thriving today and tomorrow as the City Different."</p>
</div>
<p style="margin-top:14px;">Below each topic area, the Vision Summary states a "What We'll Do" commitment. Quoted verbatim, topic by topic:</p>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><b>People &amp; Housing:</b> "Accommodate a variety of attainable housing types to retain and attract a diversity of residents." Foster belonging, respect and community pride. Strengthen connections across the diverse fabric of Santa Fe's communities and places.</li>
<li><b>History, Culture &amp; the Arts:</b> Embrace our collective heritage while incorporating change and meeting needs. Broaden the range of contributors to advance innovation and expression. Nurture the development and exhibition of arts and culture in public, private and institutional settings.</li>
<li><b>Engagement &amp; Governance:</b> Provide a variety of engagement channels to understand and respond to community, business and provider needs. Collaborate internally and across agencies. Operate in a fiscally sustainable and transparent manner.</li>
<li><b>Education, Social &amp; Economic Opportunity:</b> Align education and workforce development with community and economic needs. Diversify the economy to grow individual and community prosperity. Strengthen social networks and community capacity.</li>
<li><b>Nature, Sustainability &amp; Resiliency:</b> Preserve, enhance and manage natural landscapes and water resources for many compatible uses. Protect environmental quality. Adapt to climate and socioeconomic changes.</li>
<li><b>Community Services &amp; Essential Utilities:</b> Maintain a variety of widely accessible recreational amenities. Collaborate with partners on learning, health and social services. Continuously modernize utilities infrastructure.</li>
<li><b>Transportation &amp; Access to Opportunity:</b> Create connected, comfortable and safe transportation corridors for all users. Provide transportation services that responsibly meet mobility needs. Align transportation investments to maximize socioeconomic, environmental and placemaking benefits.</li>
<li><b>The Built City:</b> Provide pattern and design flexibility, creativity and excellence befitting of every context and scale. Distribute activities throughout the city to broaden access to amenities and services. Use quality public and private investment to address needs and opportunities.</li>
</ul>
</section>

